.TH GPIO 3
.SH NAME
gpio \- general-purpose I/O pins and their interrupts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #γ /dev
.PP
.B /dev/gpio/ctl
.B /dev/gpio/event
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The PC's GPIO controller
(AMD's, ACPI's
.B AMDI0030
or
.BR AMD0030 ,
found at boot)
gives its pins to the programs that drive what is wired to them:
.IR acpi (8)
runs the firmware's methods for the pins of
.B _AEI
(a laptop's power button),
.IR i2chid (8)
reads a touchpad when its pin
.RB ( GpioInt )
signals.
.PP
Written, the control file
.B ctl
takes:
.TF "\fIn\fP int level|edge high|low|both"
.TP
.IB n " int level|edge high|low|both"
pin
.IR n 's
interrupt, triggered by its level or an edge, active high, low or both.
It is masked when it comes, until a reader of
.B event
acknowledges it.
.TP
.IB n " out 0|1"
drive pin
.IR n .
.TP
.IB n " off"
neither.
.PD
.PP
Read, it gives a line
.IP
.BI pin " n use mode polarity level register"
.PP
per pin in use
.RI ( use :
.B int
or
.BR out ;
.IR level :
the pin's input, 0 or 1;
.IR register :
the pin's register as it is, in hex)
and the number of interrupts in a last line,
.BI interrupts " n" \fR.
.PP
Each open of
.B event
hears of its own pins.
Written,
.BI watch " n ..."
adds pins to them and unmasks their interrupts,
.BI ack " n"
unmasks pin
.IR n 's
interrupt again (a level stays until its cause is handled: ack after
handling it).
A read blocks until one of its pins' interrupts has come and returns a line
.IB n \en
per such pin.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/pc/devgpio.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acpi (8),
.IR i2chid (8)
.SH HISTORY
Written for 9space.
The Raspberry Pi's
.B #G
(9front's
.BR /sys/src/9/bcm/devgpio.c )
has the same files with other messages:
its event file is one reader's, a mask of 32 pins;
this controller has 184 and interrupts by level, acknowledged.
